Diane checked her watch against the facilities recorder. “Baseline begins at four-oh-seven.”

Miriam had turned away from them.

She faced the service opening. A plywood panel once covered it, but maintenance had removed the panel years earlier after repeated moisture complaints. The opening began less than three feet above the floor. Cooler air gathered at its mouth, carrying the mineral smell of damp concrete and old insulation. Beyond it, a concrete ledge narrowed into a low void beneath the old stage-storage addition. Pipes crossed the first several feet. Fiberglass insulation sagged between joists farther back, gray where dust had collected along the torn edges. The ledge remained undisturbed except for recent shoe and knee marks.

Miriam removed one glove and touched two fingers to the block wall beside the opening.

“We have not finished the room record,” Diane said.

“I understand.” Miriam kept her fingers against the paint.

“What are you doing?”

“Checking my first impression.”

“With touch?”

“With attention.”

Diane watched Miriam’s hand. Her fingers stayed still against the paint. She breathed once through her nose and looked into the void as though waiting for her sight to adjust.

Evan raised his camera again.

Abby continued the baseline. “No unusual meter reading. No reported sound. Supply fan audible from the west duct. Foot traffic overhead.”

A basketball struck the fieldhouse floor above them, faint but distinct through the structure.

Miriam’s assistant unfastened the canvas case.

“Leave everything inside until the baseline is complete,” Diane said.

The younger woman closed the flap. “All right.”

Evan moved closer to the opening. “This section was outside the first search. We stayed in the main rooms because that’s where the voices were.”

Abby said, “Second prior-case disclosure.”

“I am describing access.”

“You added voices.”

Diane looked at Miriam. “Had you heard that before?”

“No.”

Abby wrote in her notebook.

Evan’s jaw tightened. He pointed into the opening. “There are marks in the dust.”

Diane crossed the room and crouched without touching the ledge. Two parallel smears ran beneath the lowest pipe, each as wide as a knee. A partial shoe tread appeared nearer the edge. The dust around both marks contained fresh fibers from the exposed insulation.

“Maintenance inspected the condensate line last month,” she said. “The work order required entry.”

“How far back?” Evan asked.

“Far enough to reach the elbow.”

He angled his light across the concrete. The beam caught foil tape, a rusted pipe hanger, and the pale edges of insulation. Several feet in, the floor dropped beyond the reach of the light.

Miriam lowered her hand from the wall.

“There are people in there,” she said.

No one spoke.

The supply fan moved air behind them. Abby’s camera fan whirred close to Diane’s shoulder. Water passed through the pipe behind the shelving in three uneven pulses, paused long enough for Diane to expect the line had emptied, then moved once more. Miriam remained beside the opening, mouth relaxed, eyes narrowed toward the void. Nothing shifted beyond the first pipe.

“People living in the building?” Diane asked.

“No.”

“Dead people?”

Miriam looked into the service opening before answering. “I would not use that description.”

Evan stepped nearer. “What would you use?”

“Something nonhuman.”

The assistant faced the dark behind the pipes.

Diane kept her attention on Miriam. “What produced that conclusion?”

“The number of impressions in a space too small for them. The way they withdraw when I direct attention toward them. Human dead usually present as individuals, even when several remain together. This feels crowded and concealed.”

“Feels where?”

“Across my face. Behind my eyes. Along the roof of my mouth.” Miriam touched the side of her jaw. “Those are the terms I use for my own perception. I am not offering them as measurements.”

Abby recorded the statement and the time.

Evan lowered himself to one knee at the service opening. “Can you tell what they want?”

“Distance.”

“From us?”

“From scrutiny.”

He switched on the light attached to his phone. “That sounds convenient.”

Miriam looked down at him. “You invited me to describe what I perceive. I can stop.”

“No. Keep going.”

Diane said, “After the baseline.”

Evan leaned into the opening far enough for his shoulder to pass beneath the first pipe.

“Come back out,” Diane said.

“I’m at the edge.”

“Your head is beyond the edge.”

He withdrew several inches but remained on one knee. “We need an image of the interior.”

“Abby records the opening first. Then we choose one device and one operator.”

“I already have my phone out.”

“That is not a control.”

“It is a camera.”

Abby shifted beside them, filming Evan’s hands, the phone, and the opening. “State the model.”

Evan named it.

“Battery?”

“Seventy-two percent.”

“Flash setting?”

“Automatic.”

“Clean the lens in frame.”

Evan wiped the lens with the edge of his shirt.

Diane saw lint catch against the glass. “Use a clean cloth.”

The assistant opened the canvas case and offered a folded microfiber square. Evan took it, cleaned the lens, and held the phone beneath Abby’s camera.

“Three photographs,” Diane said. “Same position. No movement between them.”

Evan braced his wrist on the concrete ledge. His glove pressed close to the lower lens cluster. He adjusted his grip until Diane could see the glass.

Abby counted down.

The first flash filled the opening and exposed every near surface at once: gray concrete, yellow insulation, silver tape, black pipe, Evan’s hand.

The second flash followed.

On the third, Evan shifted his elbow when the pipe above him clicked.

He pulled the phone back.

“What was that?” Miriam’s assistant asked.

“Thermal movement,” Diane said. “The pipe carries hot water.”

Evan opened the image strip. The first photograph showed the ledge and the nearest joists. The second showed more of the right wall, with a pale flare across the lower corner. The third appeared black except for a narrow white crescent near one edge.

He stared at it, then turned the phone toward the group.

“Look.”

Miriam’s assistant leaned close. Miriam stayed where she was.

“The flash fired,” Evan said. “You can see it on the edge.”

Diane held out her hand. “Give me the phone.”

“It came out black.”

“I can see that.”

“The other two are fine.”

“Give me the phone.”

He placed it in her palm.

The black frame contained little visible detail. The white crescent could have been flash glare, a lit edge of insulation, or reflection from the lens cover. Diane increased the screen brightness and zoomed. Compression blocks emerged in the center. A faint reddish patch occupied the lower left corner.

“Your glove was close to the lens,” she said.

“It was clear before the shot.”

“You moved when the pipe clicked.”

“Not enough to cover the whole camera.”

“You changed your grip. Abby’s video will show how far.”

Evan looked toward Abby. “How much of it is in frame?”

“I have your hand and part of the phone. The pipe blocks the upper angle.”

“So there is no explanation.”

“There is an incomplete record,” Diane said.

She handed the phone to Abby rather than returning it to Evan. “Record the image number, creation time, file size, flash metadata if the phone provides it, and the two frames on either side. Airplane mode. No editing, messaging, or upload until we copy the originals.”

Evan rose. “You saw the flash.”

“I saw light in the opening.”

“The photograph recorded the flash.”

“It recorded a white crescent.”

“Against a black frame.”

“Yes.”

Miriam stepped nearer but kept both feet outside the disturbed dust. “May I see it?”

Abby held the screen where Miriam could view it without taking the phone.

Miriam studied the three images in order. Her eyes returned to the black frame.

“I cannot tell you what covered the camera,” she said.

Evan’s shoulders dropped slightly.

Then Miriam looked into the service opening.

“I can tell you the place feels occupied.”

Evan turned his production camera toward her. “By the nonhuman presences.”

“That is my interpretation.”

“And the image happened where you sensed them.”

“The image happened after I directed you there.”

“That is corroboration.”

Diane rose from her crouch. Dust marked one knee of her work pants. She brushed it once and stopped when loose fibers lifted into Abby’s light.

“We have one anomalous frame after the operator changed position,” she said. “We also have two ordinary frames from the same sequence.”

Evan kept the camera on Miriam. “What are they doing in there?”

Miriam closed her eyes for a moment. Her assistant watched her face.

“They remain where people approach with fear,” Miriam said. “They press against that fear and keep it active. In my practice, some presences survive through attention, conflict, or terror. I believe these draw sustenance from frightened people.”

“How many?” Evan asked.

“More than I can separate.”

“Are they demons?”

“I have no name for them.”

“Are they dangerous?”

“They can keep a person frightened. That can cause harm whether the source is spiritual, psychological, or both.”

The assistant had pulled the folded blanket higher against her chest and stood farther from the opening than before.

Abby noticed too. She lowered the camera enough to include the assistant’s feet and the distance she had created.

Evan spoke toward his lens. “Miriam identified a cluster of nonhuman entities in the crawlspace. Immediately afterward, a photograph taken with flash produced a black frame.”

“Stop,” Diane said.

He lowered the camera a fraction. “What?”

“You reversed part of the sequence. The photograph came after she described occupancy. The nonhuman classification came after the image.”

“She said people were in there.”

“She rejected the word people after I asked her to define it. Record the complete exchange.”

“It is recording.”

“Then state the sequence accurately.”

Evan faced the black opening. “Miriam sensed multiple presences. A photograph came out black. She then clarified that the presences felt nonhuman.”

“And the operator moved during the third exposure,” Abby said.

“And the operator moved,” Evan added.

The assistant released a slow breath.

From deeper in the service void came a soft tick, followed by another several feet to the right. Diane recognized pipe contraction traveling through the hangers.

Miriam turned toward the sound.

“Hot-water line,” Diane said.

“I heard it.”

“You looked toward it.”

“Yes.” Miriam opened her eyes. “Hearing a pipe does not change what I perceive.”

Miriam turned back toward the opening. Diane pointed to the wall thermometer Abby had placed on the shelving.

“Complete the baseline.”

Abby read the temperature and humidity. She documented the phone in airplane mode, then sealed neither the device nor the image because Evan still needed the phone for emergency contact. Instead she wrote the restrictions across the top of a fresh evidence page and asked him to initial them. He signed without looking at Diane.

Miriam’s assistant knelt beside the canvas case. “May I take out the candles?”

“No flame in the basement,” Diane said.

“They are battery candles.”

“Keep them off until the room record ends.”

The assistant nodded and left them inside.

Evan retrieved the handheld meter from the floor drain. “We should test the opening.”

Diane checked Abby’s camera angle. “First reading from here. Then one at the ledge. Same operator. Meter stays visible.”

Evan held it at chest height. The green indicator remained on. The display moved between zero and one-tenth.

Abby recorded the reading.

“Now the ledge,” Diane said.

Evan crouched and extended the meter toward the opening without passing the first pipe. Abby moved to keep his hand and the display in frame.

The indicator went dark.

Evan pressed the power button.

Nothing changed.

He pressed it again, longer this time.

The meter remained dark.

